%!PS-Adobe-3.0
%%Title: surfacekit.ps — paper, canvas, and print-surface textures
%%Creator: pscat — issue #51
%%EndComments
% @requires: (lib/artkit.ps) run

% An eighth sibling library: deterministic, seeded, stylized-vector
% surface textures — paper grain, paper fibers, canvas weave,
% scratches/scuffs, and print-registration imperfections — meant to
% sit *under* other marks (dry brush, watercolor, collage, print work)
% the way a real surface sits under ink. This is not raster bump-map
% or physical-simulation texture; every preset is ordinary vector
% drawing, reproducible bit-for-bit under a fixed `/Seed`.
%
% Four of the five presets (`grain`, `fiber`, `scuff`, `misreg`) are
% thin wrappers over `lib/artkit.ps`'s `scatter` (issue #48) — the
% same "no new placement engine" choice `lib/stipplekit.ps` (issue
% #50) already made. A region built with `screct`/`scpath` is the
% first operand, exactly as `scatter`/`stipple` take it; every option
% below that isn't listed for a given preset is `scatter`'s own,
% forwarded unchanged: `/Count`, `/Density`, `/MinSpacing`, `/Seed`,
% `/Tries`, `/Budget`, `/Scale`, `/Rotate`, `/Mark`, `/Weight`. Unlike
% `stipple`, none of these four repurpose `/Weight` or give `/Density`
% a second (callback) meaning — a texture's "how strong" knob is the
% new `/Strength` option below, not a per-point tone callback, so
% there is no option-conflict surface to police here the way
% `stipple` has to.
%
% The fifth, `weave`, is *not* scatter-based — a basket weave is a
% regular grid, not a random scatter, so it walks its own grid across
% the region's `/BBox` and tests each cell's center with `scin`
% (artkit.ps) the same way `scatter` tests a candidate point: inside
% only by that one test, not by an exact clip. That is deliberate and
% consistent with every scatter-based preset here (and with `scatter`
% itself) — a mark can overhang a curved or concave region's true
% boundary by up to its own size. A caller who needs an exact edge
% clips the ambient graphics state first, the same mechanism
% `lib/hatchkit.ps`'s own `hatch` relies on:
%
%   gsave
%   newpath 100 100 moveto 300 100 300 260 260 300 curveto closepath clip
%   0 0 612 792 screct << /Density 0.02 /Seed 3 >> grain
%   grestore
%
% `examples/surfacekit.ps`'s last panel demonstrates exactly this.
%
% New options this file owns, shared by all five presets:
%
%   /Color [r g b]   each component in [0,1]; a per-preset default in
%                     the muted range real, undyed material sits in.
%   /Strength num    0..1, default 0.5 (0.35 for `misreg`). How
%                     strongly a mark reads against the paper:
%                     `Strength 0` is invisible (paper white),
%                     `Strength 1` is `/Color` at full saturation.
%                     This file assumes a light ground — every blend
%                     lerps *toward white*, not toward transparent
%                     (deliberately not `setalpha`: real Ghostscript
%                     has no PostScript-callable alpha operator at
%                     all, the same portability constraint
%                     `lib/paintkit.ps`'s watercolor section
%                     documents, and this is stylized print/paper
%                     texture, not glazed paint). A caller texturing
%                     *over* dark artwork should pass a light `/Color`
%                     directly rather than expecting `Strength` to
%                     fade toward transparent.
%
% `scatter`'s own per-mark `scale` (from `/Scale [lo hi]`, default
% [1 1]) already gives every mark a random size a caller can shape —
% `grain`/`fiber`/`scuff` reuse that *same* draw to also drive shade (a
% bigger fleck reads a little darker) and, for `fiber`/`scuff`, length.
% That is a deliberate simplification, not an oversight: it means
% every preset's visual variation rides entirely on the one random
% draw `scatter` already makes and validates, so none of these default
% `/Mark` procedures consumes any of `scatter`'s random stream
% themselves — the same "no new placement arithmetic" discipline
% `stipple` documents, extended to shading. `misreg`'s nested rings
% shade by ring position instead (isotropic, no `scale`-tied length).
% None of `grain`/`fiber`/`scuff` validate `/Scale` themselves, even
% though they read it: `scatter` validates every option it owns,
% `/Scale` included, before invoking any mark at all, so by the time a
% default mark here reads it, a bad value has already been rejected
% under `scatter`'s own error name — validating it a second time here
% would either duplicate that check or, worse, disagree with it
% (report a different name depending on whether a caller overrode
% `/Mark`, since an override skips this file's own preprocessing
% entirely but never touches `scatter`'s). Each default mark reads
% `/Scale` fresh off the shared options dict on every call rather than
% caching it once, for exactly that reason — but a shallow dict `copy`
% still shares the `/Scale` *array itself* with whatever the caller
% passed in, so `grain`/`fiber`/`scuff` each call `sfscalesnapshot`
% once, before `scatter` ever runs, to replace it with a private copy:
% a `/Weight` callback that closes over the caller's own array and
% mutates it in place would otherwise corrupt what a later mark reads,
% after `scatter`'s own validation of the *original* values already
% ran and cannot re-run.
% `weave`, having no `scatter` draw to ride on, seeds its own stream
% when `/Seed` is given, using the save/restore-via-`rrand` convention
% `hatch` already established, and only for a small per-cell shade
% jitter — its grid geometry itself is always exactly regular, seeded
% or not.
%
% Each preset's whole call (after its options are validated) runs
% inside its own `gsave`/`grestore` — every default mark sets color
% and/or line width, and without this that state would leak into
% whatever the caller does next, the same reason `hatch` wraps its own
% drawing loop. A caller-supplied `/Mark` override runs inside that
% same `gsave`/`grestore` too, for the same reason (`weave` has no
% `/Mark` option at all — it never wraps a caller-supplied drawing
% callback, so there is nothing to run inside its `gsave` but its own
% code). Every default mark still `newpath`s before drawing, and
% `weave` does too, once, before its own grid loop: a region built via
% `scpath` deliberately leaves its flattened path behind (`scatter`'s
% own docs), so a mark that skipped `newpath` would fill or stroke
% that leftover outline on its very first call only — `stipple`'s own
% header documents the exact same footgun, so it's stated here too
% rather than left to be rediscovered.
%
% Reproducibility, the mark budget, and Ghostscript compatibility for
% `grain`/`fiber`/`scuff`/`misreg` are entirely `scatter`'s own,
% unchanged. `weave` has no `/Budget` to inherit, so it pre-flights its
% own: a grid at a pathologically small `/Pitch` is rejected by
% `/MaxThreads` (a cap on cell count, cheap to check for any region)
% *and*, only when the region is a `scpath` path region (not a plain
% `screct` rectangle), a second `/MaxEdgeSamples` cap on cell count
% times edge count. Both checks matter: `scin`'s cost against a
% `/Kind /rect` region is O(1) (a bare bbox test), but against a
% `/Kind /path` region it is linear in that region's edge count
% (artkit.ps's own `scarea` documents the same asymmetry for its
% measurement pass) — a cell-count cap alone bounds neither case
% together, since a modest grid over a many-thousand-edge path region
% can still cost far more work than the cell count alone suggests.
%
% Scratch prefix: `sf-`, checked against every other sibling's own
% (`sc-`/`sq-`/`si-` in artkit.ps, `sp-` in stipplekit, `h-`/`hk-` in
% hatchkit, `pk-` in paintkit) — none collide. A caller-supplied
% `/Mark` override must not touch `sf-` names, the same discipline
% every sibling's own header states for its own prefix.
%
% Deliberate scope cuts: no raster bump maps, no absorption/wet-pigment
% simulation (the issue's own scope note) — and, inside that, no
% per-point tone *callback* for `/Density` the way `stipple`'s own
% `/Density` accepts one. A caller who wants a texture that fades
% across a region can already get that by clipping to a shaped region
% (the `gsave`/clip idiom above) rather than this file inventing a
% second density vocabulary on top of `scatter`'s own.

% --- shared helpers ----------------------------------------------------

% v sfisnum -> bool
% surfacekit's own copy of artkit's scisnum / stipplekit's spisnum —
% each sibling owns its copy rather than reaching into another file's
% scratch namespace (stipplekit's own header states the same choice).
% @internal
/sfisnum { type dup /integertype eq exch /realtype eq or } def

% dict key default sfgetdef -> value
% surfacekit's own copy of the wrap-the-default-in-an-array trick —
% see artkit's scgetdef / stipplekit's spgetdef for the reasoning.
% @internal
/sfgetdef {
  /sfgdd exch 1 array astore def
  /sfgdk exch def
  /sfgdt exch def
  sfgdt sfgdk known { sfgdt sfgdk get } { sfgdd 0 get } ifelse
} def

% val errproc name sfbindnum -
% Validates `val` is a number (else runs `errproc` — a literal `{
% some-self-documenting-name }` procedure, executed only on failure,
% so the failure itself is the self-documenting `undefined` error
% every other option check in this codebase raises the same way), then
% binds it to `name`. `val` travels in a private 1-element array until
% that check passes — the same wrap-before-bare-`def` discipline
% `stipple`'s own header describes (its `/DotRadius` was exactly a
% "plain numeric option that looked safe to bare-bind" that a Codex
% review caught) — collapsed into one call so each of this file's many
% scalar options doesn't repeat the wrap/check/bind sequence by hand.
% `errproc` is a manifest `{...}` literal at every call site, never a
% name bound and later referenced bare, so it carries no auto-exec risk
% of its own regardless of what `val` turns out to be.
% @internal
/sfbindnum {
  /sfbnname exch def
  /sfbnerr exch def
  1 array astore /sfbnbox exch def
  sfbnbox 0 get sfisnum not { sfbnerr exec } if
  sfbnname sfbnbox 0 get def
} def

% region errproc sfregiondef -> region
% Validates the `region` operand every preset here takes as its first
% argument: a dict whose `/Kind` is `/rect` or `/path` (`screct`/
% `scpath`'s own two shapes) — else runs `errproc`, the same
% self-documenting-error convention `sfbindnum` uses. `region` travels
% in a private 1-element array until every check passes, checked only
% via `type`/`get` (never a bare reference to the raw operand): a
% procedure passed as the region operand — `{ boom } opts grain`, say
% — would otherwise auto-execute on this file's very first bare
% reference to it, the same hazard `sfbindnum` exists to close for a
% numeric option, just on the *first* operand this time rather than a
% dict key (Codex review, PR #125). Once confirmed a well-formed
% region dict, the value is safe for the caller to bind bare from
% here on: a `dicttype` object is always pushed literally when
% referenced bare regardless of any executable attribute (the same
% reasoning `sccallable`'s own type list, artkit.ps, relies on in
% never treating `dicttype` as executable).
% @internal
/sfregiondef {
  /sfrderr exch def
  1 array astore /sfrdbox exch def
  sfrdbox 0 get type /dicttype ne { sfrderr exec } if
  sfrdbox 0 get /Kind known not { sfrderr exec } if
  sfrdbox 0 get /Kind get dup /rect eq exch /path eq or not
    { sfrderr exec } if
  sfrdbox 0 get
} def

% opts sfscalesnapshot -
% If `opts` has an array-typed `/Scale`, replaces it in place with a
% fresh copy of the same array; a non-array (or absent) `/Scale` is
% left untouched, so `scatter`'s own validation still rejects a
% malformed one exactly as if this never ran. `grain`/`fiber`/`scuff`
% each re-read `/Scale` fresh from their own private options dict on
% every mark call rather than caching it once (see each default
% mark's own comment for why), but a private dict from a shallow
% `copy` still *shares* the `/Scale` array object itself with whatever
% the caller passed in — a `/Weight` callback that closes over that
% same array and mutates it in place (a caller can always hold onto a
% reference to their own literal) would otherwise corrupt what a
% mark reads on a later call, after `scatter`'s own validation of the
% *original* values already ran and cannot re-run (Codex review, PR
% #125, round 2 — a real, reproducible bug: a `/Weight` that swaps in
% a string mid-run raised a raw `typecheck` deep in `sflerpfrac`
% instead of anything self-documenting). Called once per preset call,
% before `scatter` ever runs, so every candidate's `/Weight` — however
% many run before the first accepted `/Mark` — only ever sees this
% file's own private copy, never the caller's original.
% @internal
/sfscalesnapshot {
  /sfssopts exch def
  sfssopts /Scale known {
    sfssopts /Scale get
    dup type /arraytype eq {
      dup length array copy
      /sfsscopy exch def
      sfssopts /Scale sfsscopy put
    } { pop } ifelse
  } if
} def

% v lo hi sflerpfrac -> t   (0..1, clamped; 0.5 for a degenerate range)
% Where `v` falls inside `[lo hi]`, for reuse by both a mark's shade
% and (for `fiber`/`scuff`) its length — one random draw (`scatter`'s
% own per-mark `scale`) feeding two correlated effects rather than two
% independent ones, per this file's own header.
% @internal
/sflerpfrac {
  /sflfhi exch def /sflflo exch def /sflfv exch def
  sflfhi sflflo sub abs 1e-9 lt
    { 0.5 }
    { sflfv sflflo sub sflfhi sflflo sub div
      dup 0 lt { pop 0 } if
      dup 1 gt { pop 1 } if }
  ifelse
} def

% lo hi t sflerp1 -> v
% @internal
/sflerp1 {
  /sfl1t exch def /sfl1hi exch def /sfl1lo exch def
  sfl1lo sfl1hi sfl1lo sub sfl1t mul add
} def

% r g b strength t sfshade -   (sets the current color)
% Lerps from paper-white toward [r g b] by `strength`, scaled a little
% by `t` (0..1 — a bigger/further-along mark reads slightly stronger):
% mix = strength * (0.6 + 0.4*t), clamped to [0,1]. Not a physical
% model of anything — a single, deliberately simple formula every
% preset in this file shares so "how strong" means the same thing
% everywhere.
% @internal
/sfshade {
  /sfsht exch def /sfshst exch def
  /sfshb exch def /sfshg exch def /sfshr exch def
  /sfshmix sfshst 0.6 sfsht 0.4 mul add mul def
  sfshmix 0 lt { /sfshmix 0 def } if
  sfshmix 1 gt { /sfshmix 1 def } if
  1 sfshmix sub sfshr sfshmix mul add
  1 sfshmix sub sfshg sfshmix mul add
  1 sfshmix sub sfshb sfshmix mul add
  setrgbcolor
} def

% opts default3arr sfcolordef -> r g b
% Resolves and validates `/Color` (or `default3arr` if absent): a
% 3-element array, each component a number in [0,1]. The resolved
% value travels in a private 1-element array (`sfcdbox`) until every
% check passes, read only via `sfcdbox 0 get ... get` — never bound to
% a bare name of its own — so an executable `/Color` (`{ boom }`, a
% procedure and an `arraytype` object both) cannot auto-execute on a
% bare reference before its type is even checked (Codex review, PR
% #125: an earlier draft bound it bare first and validated after,
% which is backwards — `type /arraytype eq` alone doesn't rule out a
% procedure, since a procedure *is* an executable array). The three
% numbers this leaves on the stack are already validated and safe for
% the caller to `def` bare (numbers have no executable behavior).
% @internal
/sfcolordef {
  /sfcddef exch def
  /sfcdopts exch def
  sfcdopts /Color sfcddef sfgetdef 1 array astore /sfcdbox exch def
  sfcdbox 0 get type /arraytype ne { surfacekit-color-must-be-an-rgb-array } if
  sfcdbox 0 get length 3 ne { surfacekit-color-must-be-an-rgb-array } if
  sfcdbox 0 get 0 get sfisnum sfcdbox 0 get 1 get sfisnum and
    sfcdbox 0 get 2 get sfisnum and not
    { surfacekit-color-must-be-an-rgb-array } if
  sfcdbox 0 get 0 get dup 0 lt exch 1 gt or
  sfcdbox 0 get 1 get dup 0 lt exch 1 gt or or
  sfcdbox 0 get 2 get dup 0 lt exch 1 gt or or
    { surfacekit-color-components-must-be-0-to-1 } if
  sfcdbox 0 get 0 get sfcdbox 0 get 1 get sfcdbox 0 get 2 get
} def

% opts default sfstrengthdef -> strength
% @internal
/sfstrengthdef {
  /sfsddef exch def
  /sfsdopts exch def
  sfsdopts /Strength sfsddef sfgetdef
    { surfacekit-strength-must-be-a-number } /sfsdval sfbindnum
  sfsdval 0 lt sfsdval 1 gt or { surfacekit-strength-must-be-0-to-1 } if
  sfsdval
} def

% --- grain ---------------------------------------------------------

% x y scale angle sfgraindefaultmark -
% @internal
/sfgraindefaultmark {
  pop
  /sfmsc exch def /sfmy exch def /sfmx exch def
  newpath
  % /Scale is read fresh here rather than validated and cached up
  % front: `scatter` has already validated it (as its own option, not
  % this file's) before invoking any mark at all, so re-reading it
  % here defers all rejection to `scatter`'s own error names instead
  % of risking a second, inconsistent one -- see this file's own
  % header note on why grain/fiber/scuff don't validate /Scale
  % themselves.
  sfscopts /Scale [ 1 1 ] sfgetdef aload pop /sfgschi exch def /sfgsclo exch def
  sfmsc sfgsclo sfgschi sflerpfrac /sfgt exch def
  sfgrainR sfgrainG sfgrainB sfgrainstrength sfgt sfshade
  sfmx sfmy sfgrainradius sfmsc mul 0 360 arc
  closepath
  fill
} def

% region opts grain -
% @kind: Procedure
% @summary: Paper grain speckling — a scatter of tiny flecks. Fine vs. coarse grain is a choice of /Density and /Scale on this one preset, not two separate operators. Every option not listed here (/Count, /Density, /MinSpacing, /Seed, /Tries, /Budget, /Scale, /Rotate, /Mark, /Weight) is scatter's own (artkit.ps), forwarded unchanged.
% @example: 0 0 200 200 screct << /Density 0.3 /Scale [0.4 1.4] /Seed 3 >> grain
% @param: /Color [r g b] speck color, each in [0,1] (default a warm paper-fleck gray)
% @param: /Strength num 0..1, how strongly flecks read against the paper (default 0.5)
% @param: /Radius num > 0 base speck radius before scatter's own /Scale (default 0.6)
/grain {
  1 array astore /sfoptsbox exch def
  { grain-region-must-be-a-region } sfregiondef /sfregion exch def
  sfoptsbox 0 get type /dicttype ne { grain-opts-must-be-a-dict } if
  /sfopts sfoptsbox 0 get def
  /sfscopts sfopts length 2 add dict def
  sfopts sfscopts copy pop
  sfscopts sfscalesnapshot

  sfscopts /Mark known not {
    sfscopts [ 0.55 0.52 0.46 ] sfcolordef
      /sfgrainB exch def /sfgrainG exch def /sfgrainR exch def
    sfscopts 0.5 sfstrengthdef /sfgrainstrength exch def
    sfscopts /Radius 0.6 sfgetdef
      { grain-radius-must-be-a-number } /sfgrainradius sfbindnum
    sfgrainradius 0 le { grain-radius-must-be-positive } if
    sfscopts /Mark /sfgraindefaultmark load put
  } if

  gsave
  sfregion sfscopts scatter
  grestore
} def

% --- fiber -----------------------------------------------------------

% x y scale angle sffiberdefaultmark -
% @internal
/sffiberdefaultmark {
  /sfmang exch def
  /sfmsc exch def
  /sfmy exch def
  /sfmx exch def
  newpath
  % /Scale read fresh, not validated here -- see sfgraindefaultmark's
  % own comment for why.
  sfscopts /Scale [ 1 1 ] sfgetdef aload pop /sffschi exch def /sffsclo exch def
  sfmsc sffsclo sffschi sflerpfrac /sfft exch def
  sffiberR sffiberG sffiberB sffiberstrength sfft sfshade
  sffiberlen0 sffiberlen1 sfft sflerp1 /sfflen exch def
  sfmang cos sfflen mul 2 div /sffhx exch def
  sfmang sin sfflen mul 2 div /sffhy exch def
  /sffx0 sfmx sffhx sub def
  /sffy0 sfmy sffhy sub def
  /sffx1 sfmx sffhx add def
  /sffy1 sfmy sffhy add def
  sffx0 sffy0 moveto
  sffx1 sffy1 lineto
  sffiberwidth sfmsc mul setlinewidth
  1 setlinecap
  stroke
} def

% region opts fiber -
% @kind: Procedure
% @summary: Paper fibers — short, thin, randomly oriented strokes scattered across a region. Orientation comes from scatter's own /Rotate, not a new random draw. Every option not listed here (/Count, /Density, /MinSpacing, /Seed, /Tries, /Budget, /Scale, /Rotate, /Mark, /Weight) is scatter's own (artkit.ps), forwarded unchanged.
% @example: 0 0 200 200 screct << /Density 0.3 /Rotate [0 360] /Seed 5 >> fiber
% @param: /Color [r g b] fiber color, each in [0,1] (default a warm pale fiber tone)
% @param: /Strength num 0..1, how strongly fibers read against the paper (default 0.5)
% @param: /Length [lo hi] fiber length range in user-space units (default [3 9])
% @param: /Width num > 0 base stroke width before scatter's own /Scale (default 0.5)
/fiber {
  1 array astore /sfoptsbox exch def
  { fiber-region-must-be-a-region } sfregiondef /sfregion exch def
  sfoptsbox 0 get type /dicttype ne { fiber-opts-must-be-a-dict } if
  /sfopts sfoptsbox 0 get def
  /sfscopts sfopts length 2 add dict def
  sfopts sfscopts copy pop
  sfscopts sfscalesnapshot

  sfscopts /Mark known not {
    sfscopts [ 0.62 0.58 0.5 ] sfcolordef
      /sffiberB exch def /sffiberG exch def /sffiberR exch def
    sfscopts 0.5 sfstrengthdef /sffiberstrength exch def
    % Wrapped before any bare reference -- same reason sfcolordef's
    % own header gives: an executable `/Length` (`{ boom }`) would
    % otherwise auto-execute the instant it's referenced bare, before
    % `scokrange` (or anything else) ever got a chance to check its
    % type (Codex review, PR #125).
    sfscopts /Length [ 3 9 ] sfgetdef 1 array astore /sfflenbox exch def
    sfflenbox 0 get scokrange not
      { fiber-length-must-be-a-two-number-range } if
    /sffiberlen0 sfflenbox 0 get 0 get def
    /sffiberlen1 sfflenbox 0 get 1 get def
    sfscopts /Width 0.5 sfgetdef
      { fiber-width-must-be-a-number } /sffiberwidth sfbindnum
    sffiberwidth 0 le { fiber-width-must-be-positive } if
    sfscopts /Mark /sffiberdefaultmark load put
  } if

  gsave
  sfregion sfscopts scatter
  grestore
} def

% --- scuff -------------------------------------------------------------

% x y scale angle sfscuffdefaultmark -
% @internal
/sfscuffdefaultmark {
  /sfmang exch def
  /sfmsc exch def
  /sfmy exch def
  /sfmx exch def
  newpath
  % /Scale read fresh, not validated here -- see sfgraindefaultmark's
  % own comment for why.
  sfscopts /Scale [ 1 1 ] sfgetdef aload pop /sfuchi exch def /sfuclo exch def
  sfmsc sfuclo sfuchi sflerpfrac /sfut exch def
  sfscuffR sfscuffG sfscuffB sfscuffstrength sfut sfshade
  sfscufflen0 sfscufflen1 sfut sflerp1 /sfulen exch def
  sfmang cos /sfudx exch def
  sfmang sin /sfudy exch def
  sfudx sfulen mul 2 div /sfuhx exch def
  sfudy sfulen mul 2 div /sfuhy exch def
  /sfux0 sfmx sfuhx sub def
  /sfuy0 sfmy sfuhy sub def
  /sfux1 sfmx sfuhx add def
  /sfuy1 sfmy sfuhy add def
  % A small perpendicular kink at the midpoint -- a deterministic
  % function of the mark's own angle (sin of 3x the angle), not a new
  % random draw, so a scuff mark stays reproducible purely off
  % scatter's own seeded rotation/scale, per this file's own header.
  % Perpendicular unit vector to (dx,dy) is (-dy,dx) (a +90-degree
  % rotation), the same derivation hatchkit's own header uses.
  sfscuffkink sfulen mul sfmang 3 mul sin mul /sfukoff exch def
  /sfukx sfmx sfudy neg sfukoff mul add def
  /sfuky sfmy sfudx sfukoff mul add def
  sfux0 sfuy0 moveto
  sfukx sfuky lineto
  sfux1 sfuy1 lineto
  sfscuffwidth sfmsc mul setlinewidth
  1 setlinecap
  1 setlinejoin
  stroke
} def

% region opts scuff -
% @kind: Procedure
% @summary: Scratches and scuffs — sparse long strokes with a small deterministic kink at the midpoint, scattered across a region. Named `scuff` rather than `scratch` since this codebase already uses "scratch" as a term of art for private working state throughout every sibling library's own docs. Every option not listed here (/Count, /Density, /MinSpacing, /Seed, /Tries, /Budget, /Scale, /Rotate, /Mark, /Weight) is scatter's own (artkit.ps), forwarded unchanged.
% @example: 0 0 200 200 screct << /Count 8 /Rotate [0 360] /Seed 9 >> scuff
% @param: /Color [r g b] scuff color, each in [0,1] (default a dark scuffed tone)
% @param: /Strength num 0..1, how strongly scuffs read against the paper (default 0.5)
% @param: /Length [lo hi] scuff length range in user-space units (default [14 40])
% @param: /Width num > 0 base stroke width before scatter's own /Scale (default 0.7)
% @param: /Kink num perpendicular kink amount, a fraction of length (default 0.18)
/scuff {
  1 array astore /sfoptsbox exch def
  { scuff-region-must-be-a-region } sfregiondef /sfregion exch def
  sfoptsbox 0 get type /dicttype ne { scuff-opts-must-be-a-dict } if
  /sfopts sfoptsbox 0 get def
  /sfscopts sfopts length 2 add dict def
  sfopts sfscopts copy pop
  sfscopts sfscalesnapshot

  sfscopts /Mark known not {
    sfscopts [ 0.25 0.24 0.26 ] sfcolordef
      /sfscuffB exch def /sfscuffG exch def /sfscuffR exch def
    sfscopts 0.5 sfstrengthdef /sfscuffstrength exch def
    % Wrapped before any bare reference -- see fiber's own /Length
    % handling and sfcolordef's header for why.
    sfscopts /Length [ 14 40 ] sfgetdef 1 array astore /sfulenbox exch def
    sfulenbox 0 get scokrange not
      { scuff-length-must-be-a-two-number-range } if
    /sfscufflen0 sfulenbox 0 get 0 get def
    /sfscufflen1 sfulenbox 0 get 1 get def
    sfscopts /Width 0.7 sfgetdef
      { scuff-width-must-be-a-number } /sfscuffwidth sfbindnum
    sfscuffwidth 0 le { scuff-width-must-be-positive } if
    sfscopts /Kink 0.18 sfgetdef
      { scuff-kink-must-be-a-number } /sfscuffkink sfbindnum
    sfscopts /Mark /sfscuffdefaultmark load put
  } if

  gsave
  sfregion sfscopts scatter
  grestore
} def

% --- misreg --------------------------------------------------------

% x y scale angle sfmisregdefaultmark -
% @internal
/sfmisregdefaultmark {
  pop
  /sfmsc exch def /sfmy exch def /sfmx exch def
  0 1 sfmisregrings 1 sub {
    /sfmr exch def
    sfmr 1 add sfmisregrings div /sfmmix exch def
    sfmisregR sfmisregG sfmisregB sfmisregstrength sfmmix sfshade
    sfmisregrings sfmr sub sfmisregrings div /sfmrf exch def
    newpath
    sfmx sfmy sfmisregradius sfmsc mul sfmrf mul 0 360 arc
    closepath
    fill
  } for
} def

% region opts misreg -
% @kind: Procedure
% @summary: Subtle print/registration imperfections — sparse soft smudge blobs (nested lightening rings, largest and faintest outermost) suggesting a mis-registered ink layer. A stylized single-color mark, not a multi-plate CMY simulation. Every option not listed here (/Count, /Density, /MinSpacing, /Seed, /Tries, /Budget, /Scale, /Rotate, /Mark, /Weight) is scatter's own (artkit.ps), forwarded unchanged.
% @example: 0 0 200 200 screct << /Count 6 /Scale [0.8 1.6] /Seed 11 >> misreg
% @param: /Color [r g b] smudge color, each in [0,1] (default a cool muted tone)
% @param: /Strength num 0..1, how strongly the innermost ring reads (default 0.35)
% @param: /Radius num > 0 outermost-ring base radius before scatter's own /Scale (default 3)
% @param: /Rings int >= 1 number of nested rings (default 3)
/misreg {
  1 array astore /sfoptsbox exch def
  { misreg-region-must-be-a-region } sfregiondef /sfregion exch def
  sfoptsbox 0 get type /dicttype ne { misreg-opts-must-be-a-dict } if
  /sfopts sfoptsbox 0 get def
  /sfscopts sfopts length 2 add dict def
  sfopts sfscopts copy pop

  sfscopts /Mark known not {
    sfscopts [ 0.55 0.5 0.65 ] sfcolordef
      /sfmisregB exch def /sfmisregG exch def /sfmisregR exch def
    sfscopts 0.35 sfstrengthdef /sfmisregstrength exch def
    sfscopts /Radius 3 sfgetdef
      { misreg-radius-must-be-a-number } /sfmisregradius sfbindnum
    sfmisregradius 0 le { misreg-radius-must-be-positive } if
    sfscopts /Rings 3 sfgetdef
      { misreg-rings-must-be-a-number } /sfmisregrings sfbindnum
    sfmisregrings 1 lt { misreg-rings-must-be-at-least-1 } if
    % /Budget bounds scatter's own mark count, not per-mark cost --
    % /Rings multiplies the work *inside* every mark, so it needs its
    % own cap independent of /Budget for "mark budgets keep rendering
    % practical" to actually hold (100 is already far past any visual
    % use of nested rings). Checked *before* the integer check below,
    % as a plain real-number comparison: a finite but extreme /Rings
    % (1e300, say) is safely > 100 without ever needing conversion,
    % whereas `cvi` on a value that far outside this interpreter's i64
    % range raises a raw `rangecheck` instead of this file's own named
    % error (Codex review, PR #125, round 3).
    sfmisregrings 100 gt { misreg-rings-must-be-100-or-fewer } if
    sfmisregrings cvi sfmisregrings ne { misreg-rings-must-be-an-integer } if
    sfscopts /Mark /sfmisregdefaultmark load put
  } if

  gsave
  sfregion sfscopts scatter
  grestore
} def

% --- weave -----------------------------------------------------------

% region opts weave -
% @kind: Procedure
% @summary: Canvas weave — a regular grid of alternating over/under thread bumps across a region, testing each cell's center with scin (artkit.ps) rather than a caller-supplied mark; not scatter-based, since a weave is a fixed structure rather than a random placement. Clip the ambient graphics state first for an exact edge on a non-rectangular region (see this file's own header).
% @example: 0 0 200 200 screct << /Pitch 5 /Seed 2 >> weave
% @param: /Color [r g b] thread color, each in [0,1] (default a canvas tan)
% @param: /Strength num 0..1, how strongly threads read against the paper (default 0.5)
% @param: /Pitch num > 0 grid spacing between thread centers (default 6)
% @param: /Width num > 0 thread thickness (default 1.2)
% @param: /Seed n seeds a small per-cell shade jitter with `n srand`, restoring the caller's stream afterward via `rrand` (hatchkit's own convention) -- the grid geometry itself is always exactly regular, seeded or not
% @param: /MaxThreads int pre-flight cap on grid cell count (default 20000)
% @param: /MaxEdgeSamples int pre-flight cap on cell count times region edge count, checked only for a scpath path region (default 2000000)
/weave {
  1 array astore /sfoptsbox exch def
  { weave-region-must-be-a-region } sfregiondef /sfregion exch def
  sfoptsbox 0 get type /dicttype ne { weave-opts-must-be-a-dict } if
  /sfopts sfoptsbox 0 get def

  sfopts [ 0.5 0.42 0.3 ] sfcolordef
    /sfweaveB exch def /sfweaveG exch def /sfweaveR exch def
  sfopts 0.5 sfstrengthdef /sfweavestrength exch def

  sfopts /Pitch 6 sfgetdef
    { weave-pitch-must-be-a-number } /sfweavepitch sfbindnum
  sfweavepitch 0 le { weave-pitch-must-be-positive } if

  sfopts /Width 1.2 sfgetdef
    { weave-width-must-be-a-number } /sfweavewidth sfbindnum
  sfweavewidth 0 le { weave-width-must-be-positive } if

  sfopts /MaxThreads 20000 sfgetdef
    { weave-maxthreads-must-be-a-number } /sfweavemaxthreads sfbindnum

  sfregion /BBox get aload pop
  /sfwy1 exch def /sfwx1 exch def /sfwy0 exch def /sfwx0 exch def
  /sfwbw sfwx1 sfwx0 sub def
  /sfwbh sfwy1 sfwy0 sub def

  % Raw (real-valued) column/row counts, sanity-capped *before* any
  % `cvi`: an extreme /Pitch (say 1e-300) divided into an ordinary
  % bbox produces a real so large that converting it straight to this
  % interpreter's i64 int type would overflow well before /MaxThreads
  % below ever gets a chance to reject it -- the same overflow class
  % tests/sweep.rs's own extreme-range tests guard against elsewhere in
  % this codebase. 1e7 is far above any sane /MaxThreads and safely
  % within i64 range even after the row*col multiply further down.
  /sfwcolsf sfwbw sfweavepitch div def
  /sfwrowsf sfwbh sfweavepitch div def
  sfwcolsf 1e7 gt sfwrowsf 1e7 gt or
    { weave-cell-count-exceeds-safety-limit } if

  % Round (not ceiling) to the nearest whole cell count, then re-derive
  % the *actual* per-axis spacing (`sfwpx`/`sfwpy`) from that count
  % rather than drawing at the nominal /Pitch directly. Ceiling plus a
  % fixed /Pitch left the last row/column's center outside the bbox
  % whenever the bbox wasn't an exact multiple of /Pitch -- `scin`
  % rejects any candidate outside the bbox on its own bare bbox test,
  % so that dropped a whole edge strip silently (or, for a region
  % narrower than /Pitch/2, every cell). Rounding to fit means the
  % realized spacing can differ from /Pitch by up to half a cell, but
  % every cell's center is now guaranteed inside the bbox.
  sfwcolsf 0.5 add cvi /sfwcols exch def
  sfwrowsf 0.5 add cvi /sfwrows exch def
  sfwcols 1 lt { /sfwcols 1 def } if
  sfwrows 1 lt { /sfwrows 1 def } if
  /sfwcells sfwcols sfwrows mul def
  sfwcells sfweavemaxthreads gt { weave-cell-count-exceeds-safety-limit } if

  sfregion /Kind get /path eq {
    sfopts /MaxEdgeSamples 2000000 sfgetdef
      { weave-maxedgesamples-must-be-a-number } /sfweavemaxedges sfbindnum
    /sfwedges sfregion /Edges get length 4 idiv def
    sfwcells sfwedges mul sfweavemaxedges gt
      { weave-region-too-complex-for-this-pitch } if
  } if

  /sfwpx sfwbw sfwcols div def
  /sfwpy sfwbh sfwrows div def

  sfopts /Seed known /sfweaveseeded exch def
  sfweaveseeded { /sfweavesaved rrand def sfopts /Seed get cvi srand } if

  gsave
  newpath
  0 1 sfwrows 1 sub {
    /sfwrow exch def
    0 1 sfwcols 1 sub {
      /sfwcol exch def
      /sfwcx sfwx0 sfwcol 0.5 add sfwpx mul add def
      /sfwcy sfwy0 sfwrow 0.5 add sfwpy mul add def
      sfwcx sfwcy sfregion scin {
        sfwrow sfwcol add cvi 2 mod 0 eq /sfwover exch def
        sfweaveseeded { frnd 0.1 mul 0.05 sub } { 0 } ifelse /sfwjit exch def
        sfwover
          { sfweaveR sfweaveG sfweaveB sfweavestrength sfwjit 0.75 add sfshade }
          { sfweaveR sfweaveG sfweaveB sfweavestrength sfwjit 0.35 add sfshade }
        ifelse
        newpath
        sfwover {
          sfwcx sfwpx 0.9 mul 2 div sub sfwcy sfweavewidth 2 div sub moveto
          sfwpx 0.9 mul 0 rlineto
          0 sfweavewidth rlineto
          sfwpx 0.9 mul neg 0 rlineto
          closepath
        } {
          sfwcx sfweavewidth 2 div sub sfwcy sfwpy 0.9 mul 2 div sub moveto
          sfweavewidth 0 rlineto
          0 sfwpy 0.9 mul rlineto
          sfweavewidth neg 0 rlineto
          closepath
        } ifelse
        fill
      } if
    } for
  } for
  grestore
  sfweaveseeded { sfweavesaved srand } if
} def
